# k31rel. The same one request as docs/cards/k31-nvme-regime.card, with the NVMe expert tier's own reader in place of the mapping's
# faults: BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=32G makes the K3.1 bp plan carry a RAM arena (the split's default gives 0 at this room), so the
# experts the host does not lock are read by NvTier (O_DIRECT pread, 16 reader threads, one read per expert part of ~2 MiB, the
# arena an LRU of ~5,100 experts at 6.25 MB) instead of ~94 KiB faults at a queue of ~5. The host's locked share drops by the arena
# (249 → ~217 GB), so the paged share rises from 259 to ~291 GB. No code changes. The mirror round (the set on both drives, the
# reader picking the drive per read) attaches to this reader, so this probe prices that round's base before any code is written.
kind: calibration
question: with a 32 GiB arena, what decode rate does the same K3.1 bp request reach, and does the tier's reader keep the drive busier than the faults did?
term: decode tok/s @ D=512, n_predict 128, one cold request, with iostat nvme0n1's decode-phase rareq-sz, aqu-sz and rMB/s beside it
unit: tok/s
predict: 5.5..9.0
condition: tools/ref/k31-nvme-probe.sh with K31_PROBE_ENV=BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=32G (the 0.3.13 sm86 tarball's bloomery-serve,
  BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=252G, --place bp --ctx-size 2048, the first 512 prose ids, n_predict 128, iostat beside it) under one lease
  after the read_ahead A/B's, read_ahead_kb back at 128; the plan line must print arena=34359738368 (else the lever did not reach
  the plan and the row is not read), and its host_experts= and paged= are recorded beside the regime probe's (76910, 259192872960)
reason: [derived] the regime probe's step is 305 ms, of which ~270 ms is ~2,200 faults of ~94 KiB in series at a queue of ~5
  (r_await 0.34 ms, concurrency ~2.4-6); the reader issues a miss expert's three ~2 MiB parts at once through 16 threads, so a
  layer with one miss costs ~1 ms (6 MiB at 6.4 GB/s plus one latency) and a token of ~72 misses over 92 layers ~70-100 ms of
  NVMe in series with ~30 ms of DRAM and ~10 ms of cards: 110-140 ms = 7-9 tok/s at the regime probe's 0.45 GB a token. The term
  derivation cannot fix is the arena's hit rate against the page cache's: the probe's 0.45 GB a token is 4.9 % of the picks
  missing, far under the 43 % paged share, so the cache re-serves most paged picks; a 32 GiB LRU of the hottest paged experts
  must do the same or the NVMe bytes rise toward 4 GB a token (625 ms at 6.4 GB/s, slower than today). The band's low end is a
  partial hit rate, its high end the full one
decide-in: the explicit-read path is the K3.1 decode path: the mirror round lands on NvTier (the sidecar's directories opened per
  shard, the drive with fewer reads in flight picked per read) and the serving docs name BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES for K3.1 until
  the split's default plans the arena for it; the mirror's prediction is this row's NVMe term halved
decide-below: under 5.5: read the bytes a token (/proc/<pid>/io) first: above ~1 GB the LRU misses what the page cache held
  (the arena is too small or its victim rule is wrong for K3.1's routing) and the next lever is the arena size (64G, 96G) or a
  fadvise prefetch on the mapping instead of the reader; near 0.45 GB with a low rate the reader's queue is not the drive's
  (read the reader stats record by kind) before the mirror
decide-above: above 9.0: the DRAM or card term was overestimated; re-read the timeline from the record before the mirror's prediction
# outcome 2026-11-07 (one request, arena=34359738368 arena_slots=56, host_experts 76910 at 215 GB, paged 294 GB, load 52 s): tg 2.04 /
#  pp 2.09 tok/s, below the band. /proc/<pid>/io 1,026 GB over 640 positions = 1.6 GB a position (the mapping's run: 0.65) with 730
#  major faults: the reader took every miss from the drive (the tier drops a paged stack's pages after each read; the arena is
#  staging, not a hot set), where the mapping path's page cache re-served most paged picks. iostat decode: aqu-sz 15, rareq-sz
#  624 KiB, 3,254 MB/s, r_await 2.79 ms, util 54 %: the queue and the read size the reader was built for, and the step (491 ms)
#  = 1.6 GB / 3.25 GB/s, so at this depth the drive gives ~3.3 GB/s on ~624 KiB random reads, not its 6.4 sequential. decide-below's
#  first case: the bytes. The K3.1 path keeps the mapping (its hits) and gains the reader's depth by prefetching exactly a layer's
#  miss runs into the page cache after the router (posix_fadvise WILLNEED per part, every pick, the mirror chosen by expert parity
#  so the cache holds each expert once): [derived] 0.45 GB a token at ~3.3 GB/s = ~140 ms + ~40 ms = ~5.5 tok/s on one drive,
#  ~70 + 40 = ~9 tok/s over two. The mirror round (k31mirror) is redirected to that design; NvTier is untouched.
